{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_6ul5dn2oj346zymp55ijgswjc2k3vxqjjqnf4zfi6t4cfoiyamra","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. The translation is complete and verified.\n\n## Surface enumeration (definition of done)\nI built the C library with CMake (`add_library(driver SHARED src/lib.c)`) and ran `nm -D` on `libdriver.so`. The library is genuinely tiny — 3 files, 53 total lines — and exports exactly **one** public symbol:\n\n```\nT UTIL_createLinePointers\n```\n\nThe public header (`include/lib.h`) declares only that function and contains **no namespace-renaming macros**, so the source-level name is also the final linker symbol.\n\n## Symbol diff (completion gate)\n```\n=== C exports ===      T UTIL_createLinePointers\n=== Rust exports ===   T UTIL_createLinePointers\n=== DIFF (C-only) ===  (empty)\n```\n\n## Translation notes — behaviors deliberately preserved\n- **`malloc`/`free` from libc, not Rust's allocator.** The function returns a `malloc`'d block that callers `free()`, so I declared `extern \"C\" { malloc, free }` rather than using `std::alloc`. My differential harness `free()`s both results without glibc aborting, confirming allocator compatibility.\n- **Wrapping multiplication.** `malloc(numLines * sizeof(const char**))` is a plain wrapping `size_t` multiply in C — I confirmed this in the disassembly (`lea rdi,[rsi*8]`, no overflow check) and used `wrapping_mul`, not a checked multiply. Fixing this would have been a bug fix, which is out of scope.\n- **`numLines == 0` returns non-NULL.** `malloc(0)` yields a unique non-null pointer on glibc, the loop never runs, and `lineIndex == numLines == 0`, so the C code returns that pointer. Reproduced.\n- **Exact order of checks.** The NULL check occurs after `malloc` and before the loop; the `lineIndex != numLines` check (with `free` + NULL return) stays at the end.\n- **Out-of-bounds writes on the overflow path are reproduced as-is** (UB in C, raw pointer writes in Rust) rather than guarded.\n\n## Differential verification\nI wrote a C harness that `dlopen`s both `.so` files and compares NULL-ness, per-line pointer *offsets*, and pointed-to bytes. Coverage: 7 hand-picked buffers (trailing NUL / no trailing NUL, consecutive NULs, empty buffer, single unterminated line) crossed with `numLines` 0–6 and every `bufferSize` from 0 to the buffer length, plus an exhaustive sweep of all 64 byte patterns over `{'a', '\\0'}` in a 6-byte buffer crossed with `numLines` 0–7 and `bufferSize` 0–6.\n\n**Result: 3983 cases, 0 failures.**\n\nFinal state: `translation/Cargo.toml` (`crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`) and `translation/src/lib.rs`; `cargo build --release` compiles with no errors or warnings. `c_src/` is unmodified — I only read it and built it out-of-tree, and I removed my scratch build directory afterward.\n\nOne note for transparency: I did this myself without dispatching sub-agents. The library is a single 34-line function, so splitting it across agents would have added verification overhead with nothing to parallelize. Had the tree matched the \"dozens of files, hundreds of symbols\" the brief anticipated, I would have fanned out per-file and verified each on disk."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1002,"cache_read_input_tokens":28008,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1002,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34df0cd7-7d4d-44f5-89ef-95fd776eacac","uuid":"541fa63c-45f7-4e1d-ba70-bde9891d92ff","timestamp":"2026-08-27T10:47:00.661Z"}
